# The Nexus

The `runtime` provider is the **nexus** — the optional server tier behind the Dock,
where the trusted and long-lived work lives:

- **agents** — the analyst, coder, researcher, reflexion loops, in native server Elixir;
- **data & sync** — the canonical store and the diffs that drive live SSR;
- **the compiler lane** — turning a Svelte island or a Rust unit into wasm;
- **multi-tenancy** — keeping one customer's run off another's.

A workbook can ship with *no* nexus: the static bundle hydrates in the browser, and
any runtime-only request just has no provider, so those units stay inert. Add a
nexus and the same workbook gains agents, sync, and server SSR — no rewrite. The
contract didn't change; a second provider showed up to fulfill it.

server :outreach do
  def draft(%Lead{} = lead) do
    # a runtime-only request: no provider in a static bundle, the agent loop when
    # a nexus is attached. Same code either way.
    Dock.agent(:analyst, lead: lead, ask: "draft a first-touch email")
  end
end

## The generated WIT — shown once, never written

Everything here compiles to a standard **wasm component**, so an Elixir unit, a Rust
[[Forecast]] kernel, and a Python [[Risk]] scorer all interoperate and all stay
sandboxed. The component's low-level contract is a **WIT world** — and this is the
only place in the workbook you'll see one, shown so you trust you never write it:

// GENERATED — you never touch this. Emitted by the weave from the :tax module.
world tax {
  record money { cents: s64, currency: string }
  variant tax-result { ok(money), err(tax-error) }
  enum tax-error { no-rate }
  export tax: func(subtotal: money, region: string) -> tax-result;
  import host:net/fetch;
}

Every line maps back to Elixir you already wrote — the public `tax/2`, the `Money`
struct, the grant. The weave derives it; if you ever "fix" the WIT, you're editing
the wrong thing. **Learn Elixir, not WIT.**
